= ChromaForge GM handbook
For whoever runs the game, including a solo player running it for themselves. Rules for checks, combat, magic, and advancement live in the player handbook; this handbook covers what the GM decides, generates, and tracks.

Sections marked #strong[Draft] are new and untested. Sections marked #strong[Open] wait on a design decision.

#art-box(88, 55, "GM at the table")
== Principles
- #strong[Founts are laws without agency.] They cannot want, permit, punish, or plan. People and creatures have motives; a Fount effect has a reliable, discoverable rule.
- #strong[Roll only when the outcome is uncertain, risky, and consequential.] Name the risk and consequence before the roll.
- #strong[Clues are never lost.] Correct investigation always reveals the essential clue. A check decides speed, safety, completeness, or extra leverage, never whether play stops.
- #strong[Generated facts are canon] once they reach the table. Re-roll only before reveal, or when a result contradicts established Katharra canon.
- #strong[Fights need a motive and an exit.] Every enemy wants something and has a condition that ends the fight short of death.
- #strong[The world moves.] Ignored leads advance; revisited places show what changed.

== Start a session in ten minutes
+ Each player makes a character from the player handbook.
+ Roll every step of the starting-location generator. Read the visible situation, not hidden causes.
+ Pick matching names, creatures, or places from the Katharra alpha pool when useful. Generated details may fill canon gaps but cannot contradict listed facts.
+ Ask, "What do you do?" Use a check only when failure has an interesting consequence.
+ At scene end, record changed facts, advance ignored leads, and award obstacle points.

== Scene loop
For each new place or changed revisit, write six short lines:

+ #strong[See:] immediate sensory facts.
+ #strong[Want:] who wants what; never assign desire to a Fount.
+ #strong[Rule:] primary Fount's reliable effect; secondary twist if generated.
+ #strong[Ignored:] what changes if the party leaves or follows another lead.
+ #strong[Roll:] only risk, consequence, Fount, and difficulty, not a planned solution.
+ #strong[Leads:] at least one actionable next step; starting locations have two.

Then:

+ Show #strong[See] and the visible problem.
+ Ask what characters do. Resolve fiction first, then checks or combat.
+ Preserve essential clues despite failure.
+ When scene stakes settle, award points and advance time.
+ Tick or rewrite every unfollowed lead's #strong[Ignored] line.
+ Save names, rules, relationships, damage, and consequences. On revisit, reuse them and show what changed.

== Rulings
=== Which Fount
Choose the Fount matching the method, not the desired outcome. The same goal can use any Fount:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (auto, 37fr, 29fr,),
    align: (left,left,left,),
    table.header([Fount], [Methods], [Getting past a guard],),
    table.hline(),
    [ᛄ~Seron], [rules, oaths, edges, standing firm, honest claims], [cite the law that says you may pass],
    [∵~Orvik], [study, memory, analysis, patterns, careful aim], [learn the shift change and slip through],
    [ϟ~Zhark], [improvising, breaking, sudden change, speed], [cause a distraction and run],
    [△~Drun], [strength, endurance, patience, labor], [wait out the night, or push through],
    [⌘~Veyra], [persuasion, performance, bonds, deception], [befriend the guard],)]
  , kind: table
  )

Players may propose another method; if it is plausible, let the Fount follow the method.

